  "text": " So, one of the biggest topics that's come up as an organ injury syndrome with the vaccines is myocarditis. We've probably never heard the word myocarditis before the vaccines. You probably never knew the heart could have inflammation. And it's of interest because it seems to affect the children. And here we are right on the after day of approval for emergency use authorization of children age 5 to 11, despite the signal of myocarditis. It's now proven, it's proven that the spike protein alone, in alone and by itself, when it's expressed in pericyte and cardiomyocytes, causes damage to the heart, is expressed on the cell surface, the body attacks its own heart muscle cells, it causes inflammation in the heart, that causes measurable damage to the heart, obvious EKG changes, elevations in cardiac troponin, cardiac arrhythmias, incipient heart failure, and in some unfortunate children cardiac death. And it's already happened. I've already been asked to review autopsies, a few children have died of myocarditis. The FDA knows this. When the FDA and CDC reviewed this in June, they had 200 cases. 200 cases. And they disingenually said two things. It was rare. We never save that in safety work. Never. Because we didn't check everybody for it. They only had 200 cases that people brought forward. The second thing they said is it's mild. 90% of kids in the initial CDC review were hospitalized. You know what takes the hospitalized 17-year-old kid and take them out of school? No, it's not mild. So those two things were, that's basically wrongdoing. By officials and authority, they should be concerned. They should be conservative. And they should have said, listen, this is serious. These kids are being hospitalized. And this could be the tip of the iceberg. And I'm telling you, as a doctor in a position of authority, that's what should have been said. I'll be very clear with everybody about this. It's not rare and it's serious. And you know, here we are today, what did I tell you? We have 10,000 cases. They had 200. Who in the world said it was rare?",
